tion/opencode-platform/002-opencode-v1-v2-dual-compat/034-live-plugin-playbook/scratch/live-runs/dist-freshness.v1.txt:\n  Line 11: [dist-freshness-guard] Local compiled dist is stale; affected outputs may be untrustworthy until rebuilt:\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/cli-external-orchestration/opencode-platform/002-opencode-v1-v2-dual-compat/034-live-plugin-playbook/scratch/live-runs/results-matrix.md:\n  Line 21: | `system-dist-freshness-guard` | PASS | PASS | `dist-freshness.v{1,2}.txt` | a stale sk-design backend dist puts `[dist-freshness-guard] Local compiled dist is stale` in the system prompt on both; v1 logs it from `session.created`, v2 from the transform |\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/scripts/README.md:\n  Line 94: | `check-dist-staleness.sh` | Python script (kept as a `.sh` entrypoint) that checks whether a watched TypeScript package's compiled dist is stale, scoped to one edited file by default or every watched package with `--all` |\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-speckit/spec-authoring-and-continuity/003-weak-model-spec-authoring/008-playbook-run-in-worktree/scratch/runs/author.jsonl:\n  Line 97: {\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1792762793013,\"sessionID\":\"ses_e9707d19fffeMnGBXPzxvktABu\",\"part\":{\"partID\":\"prt_168fd4b94001soMflA2G4NCApO\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_e9707d19fffeMnGBXPzxvktABu\",\"messageID\":\"msg_168fd2f2e001vqQyUdUlbRNryg\",\"type\":\"tool\",\"id\":\"call_01a168fd48bd7d51866470f4\",\"tool\":\"shell\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"sed -n '1,120p' .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook/tooling-and-scripts/cli-dist-freshness-guard.md\"},\"output\":\"---\\ntitle: \\\"429 -- CLI Dist-Freshness Guard Trip\\\"\\ndescription: \\\"Manual check that a CLI shim refuses a stale dist entrypoint with exit 69 and a rebuild instruction, that the per-system dev-override env restores pass-through, and that the trip is fully reversible via content restore.\\\"\\nversion: 2.0.0.0\\nid: tooling-and-scripts-cli-dist-freshness-guard\\nexpected_workflow_mode: UNKNOWN\\nexpected_leaf_resources: []\\n---\\n\\n# 429 -- CLI Dist-Freshness Guard Trip\\n\\n## 1. OVERVIEW\\n\\n\\nA SHA256 content hash over the watched source files is written lazily into a small cache file next to the dist entry (e.g. `dist/.dist-freshness-<package>-<suffix>.json`) purely as a same-session performance short-circuit: if a later check finds the same content hash already cached, it reports fresh without re-checking mtime at all. This cache is not pre-warmed by any build script -- it is written only by the runtime check itself, and only on a fresh pass -- so a clean checkout or the first check against a given source state always falls through to the authoritative mtime comparison. One consequence worth knowing (directly verified): a bare `touch` (mtime bump, no content change) does NOT re-trip the guard once a matching cache entry already exists for that exact content, but the identical `touch` DOES trip the guard when no matching cache entry exists yet, because mtime alone decides staleness in that case.\\n\\nThe test trips the guard reversibly: it backs up the source file, appends a content change so it is genuin...\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-speckit/spec-folder-tooling/001-spec-folder-lifecycle/003-spec-folder-tooling/019-epic-follow-up-fixes/scratch/evidence/doc-381-manual-run.txt:\n  Line 744: @spec-kit/runtime dist is stale. Rebuild with: cd .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime && npm run build\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-speckit/memory-search-and-knowledge/001-graph-and-context-optimization/003-memory-and-causal-runtime/003-embedder-testing-and-architecture/005-cross-cutting-quality/005-cocoindex-install-hygiene/scratch/mcp-disconnect-rca.md:\n  Line 156: The second probe currently fails because code-graph's copied shared dist is stale. After a full rebuild or tsconfig cleanup, it should either support `ollama` or no longer contain/route through that stale copied tree.\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-speckit/memory-search-and-knowledge/003-memory-search-intelligence/003-spec-data-quality/016-validate-sh-dist-freshness-and-repo-remediation/001-dist-freshness-enforcement/spec.md:\n  Line 52: | **Handoff Criteria** | validate.sh fails closed (exit 3) only when the `system-spec-kit/mcp_server` validation orchestrator dist is stale, passes through when fresh; Claude hook and OpenCode plugin both emit non-blocking warnings; validation harnesses pass |\n\n  Line 74: - A hard backstop inside `validate.sh`'s `run_node_orchestrator()`: fail closed with exit `3` (validate.sh's existing \"system error\" code) and an explicit rebuild command when the `system-spec-kit/mcp_server` validation orchestrator dist is stale.\n\n  Line 90: - [x] `validate.sh` exits 3 with a clear rebuild message when the `system-spec-kit/mcp_server` validation orchestrator dist is stale; passes through unaffected when fresh.\n\n  Line 92: - [x] OpenCode plugin warns (never blocks) before a Bash call matching `opencode run` / `validate.sh` when watched dist is stale.\n\n  Line 98: - **Scenario 2**: **Given** watched package dist is stale, **when** Claude or OpenCode warning layers run, **then** they emit `STALE DIST WARNING` without throwing or blocking the user action.\n\n  Line 119: | REQ-005 | OpenCode plugin warns before risky swarm dispatch | `tool.execute.before` hook detects `opencode run`/`validate.sh` in a Bash command and warns (non-blocking) when any watched dist is stale |\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-speckit/memory-search-and-knowledge/003-memory-search-intelligence/003-spec-data-quality/016-validate-sh-dist-freshness-and-repo-remediation/001-dist-freshness-enforcement/plan.md:\n  Line 63: - [x] OpenCode plugin warns (non-blocking) before a Bash call matching `opencode run` / `validate.sh` when a watched dist is stale; registers a `session.created` one-time summary.\n\n  Line 78: - **`check-dist-staleness.sh`** (new, sibling to `check-comment-hygiene.sh`): reads `tool_input.file_path` from the same stdin JSON payload `claude-posttooluse.sh` already parses; if the path falls under a watched source tree and dist is stale, prints the warning banner; always exits 0.\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-speckit/memory-search-and-knowledge/003-memory-search-intelligence/003-spec-data-quality/016-validate-sh-dist-freshness-and-repo-remediation/001-dist-freshness-enforcement/implementation-summary.md:\n  Line 62: | validate.sh backstop | `.opencode/skills/system-spec-kit/scripts/spec/validate.sh` | Fails closed with exit `3` only when the `system-spec-kit/mcp_server` validation-orchestrator dist is stale; reports the exact rebuild command. |\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-speckit/spec-authoring-and-continuity/003-weak-model-spec-authoring/020-alignment-audit-fixes/scratch/briefs/A-A8.txt:\n  Line 4: The CLI workspace's local dist is stale. Rebuild it with that workspace's own build script\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-speckit/spec-authoring-and-continuity/003-weak-model-spec-authoring/020-alignment-audit-fixes/scratch/audit/lane-A-code.md:\n  Line 103: ### A8 (P2): the CLI workspace's local dist is stale for a file 054 changed\n\n  Line 106: - Evidence: `node runtime/cli/lib/dist-freshness.cjs check --package system-spec-kit/runtime/cli --entry default` prints `@spec-kit/cli dist is stale (source content differs from the attested b